Original commit body from addd0b390cede4f7ef36114161e469571d58fd37:

The two-account split moved the governance DATA out of the agent's reach
(/etc/hypergumbo-tracker) and the authority DECISION out of its reach
(resolve_actor keys on os.getuid(), which is not forgeable). It never
moved the CODE. There was one install -- an EDITABLE one, owned by the
agent, pointing into a checkout the agent must be able to write -- and
generate_human_shim positively instructed the human to `source
<repo>/.venv/bin/activate`. So every human-authority command (init,
lock, unlock, stealth, reconcile-reset, fork-setup, configure, setup)
executed code the agent could rewrite in place, including the function
that decides what human authority means.

It bites hardest during opt-in to the host-protected config, because
check 10c composes the sudo commands the human then pastes -- including
the destination path under /etc. An agent able to edit setup.py chooses
where the human root-creates the protected config, defeating that layer
at the moment of installation.

The asymmetry worth naming: a source change passes PR, CI and review
before anything runs it; an in-place edit to the checkout passes none of
them and runs on the human's next invocation. So the remedy is not
"trust the agent less" but "run a build the agent did not produce".

The shim now emits a NON-EDITABLE install under the human's own account,
pinned to a reviewed commit, and every command it prints is an absolute
path into that copy. The agent's venv is not mentioned anywhere. The SHA
is printed as a suggestion to VERIFY ON THE FORGE and explicitly not as
something to trust, because the pin is the whole mechanism: an agent
cannot make a given SHA contain different bytes.

Three tests asserting the old `source <repo>/.venv` output are gone --
they pinned the defect in place.

New check 23 (install_provenance) reports who can modify the running
code. It is documented and labelled an ACCIDENT GUARD, NOT A SECURITY
CONTROL: it lives inside the artifact it audits, so an agent that can
rewrite the package can rewrite the check. Claiming more for it would
repeat ADR-0013's original OS-enforcement error one layer up. What it
catches is the human who forgot and used the agent's venv -- the failure
the shipped guidance used to cause.

It tests OWNERSHIP as well as mode, because mode alone misses the real
case: the agent's package tree is 0755 the whole way down, nothing
group-writable, yet the agent owns every component. Root-owned
components are exempt (root is already trusted by anyone running sudo),
as are sticky world-writable directories like /tmp, where the sticky bit
confines delete and rename to the entry's owner -- without that the
check false-positives on anything under /tmp, including its own
fixtures.

Verified end to end: a non-editable install from a pinned SHA lands in
site-packages with no editable finder and drives the real repo.

